---
import ReviewShell from '../../components/ReviewShell.astro';
import EcosystemProcessRail from '../../components/EcosystemProcessRail.astro';
import { ecosystemStages } from '../../data/public-ecosystem';

const { publicSite = false } = Astro.props;
const routePrefix = publicSite ? '' : '/review';

const rooms = [
  { label: 'Research', title: 'A question being worked on in public.', copy: 'A self-directed attempt to build a research practice around cognitive scaffolds, returnability, and the stubborn question of how a person keeps complicated work alive long enough to learn from it.', href: `${routePrefix}/research/` },
  { label: 'Exocore', title: 'The workbench taking shape.', copy: 'A proposed local-first workbench, with one small public shell and a larger attempt to give difficult questions a room where they can stay open without disappearing from the map.', href: `${routePrefix}/exocore/` },
  {
    label: 'Field Journal',
    title: 'The public record of the build.',
    copy: 'Notes, design turns, and reflections for readers who want to follow the work as it changes. Read in time, or follow the connections between notes in the Field Map.',
    href: 'https://blog.hearthandcode.dev/#recent-entries',
    external: true,
    secondaryLink: { label: 'Graph View ↗', href: 'https://blog.hearthandcode.dev/field/' },
  },
  { label: 'AI & Ethics', title: 'A personal position, kept revisable.', copy: 'Working thoughts on authorship, judgment, collaboration, and the point where an AI system needs to hand the work back.', href: `${routePrefix}/ethics/` },
  { label: 'Lab', title: 'Experiments with the edges still showing.', copy: 'Open projects, adjacent interests, and small proofs that are still becoming what they are.', href: `${routePrefix}/lab/` },
  { label: 'Method', title: 'How the work stays honest.', copy: 'The public/private boundary, research posture, and simple habits that make an evolving practice inspectable without pretending it is finished.', href: `${routePrefix}/method/` },
  { label: 'Evidence Dossier', title: 'The person behind the work.', copy: 'A field-file-shaped portfolio of background, current practice, open questions, and selected public evidence.', href: `${routePrefix}/dossier/` },
];
---

<ReviewShell
  title="Hearth"
  description="The Hearth and Code public site."
  current="hearth"
  publicSite={publicSite}
>
  <div class="review-page">
    <section class="review-hero" aria-labelledby="hearth-title">
      <div class="review-hero-copy">
        <p class="review-kicker">The Hearth / an independent studio</p>
        <h1 id="hearth-title">A hearth for the questions I <em>keep coming back to.</em></h1>
        <p>Hearth &amp; Code is my independent studio for research, systems work, and the long attempt to make a real place for questions that have mattered to me for years: questions about cognition, tools, learning, music, and why an idea can feel obvious one day and unreachable the next.</p>
        <p>I did not arrive here through a neat institutional path. I am building the research practice I needed, slowly and in public, with enough structure to take the work seriously and enough room to follow an unexpected connection when it turns out to matter.</p>
        <div class="review-actions">
          <a class="review-button is-primary" href={`${routePrefix}/dossier/`}>Meet the person behind the work</a>
          <span class="review-journal-actions" aria-label="Field Journal reading views">
            <span>Field Journal</span>
            <a href="https://blog.hearthandcode.dev/#recent-entries" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Article List ↗</a>
            <a href="https://blog.hearthandcode.dev/field/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Graph View ↗</a>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="review-hero-aside" aria-label="What this site is for">
        <p class="review-label">Why this room exists</p>
        <h2>Curiosity deserves a room sturdy enough to work in.</h2>
        <p>This is a place for the questions that keep resurfacing: how people learn, how they make, how they organize knowledge, and how tools can carry some of the context without quietly becoming the author of someone else’s life.</p>
        <ul class="review-mini-list">
          <li><strong>Human story</strong>One person learning in public, including the false starts and quiet exits.</li>
          <li><strong>Research story</strong>Questions, sources, and a practice being built from the ground up.</li>
          <li><strong>Making story</strong>Small public artifacts, open repositories, and enough rough edge to see how the work got made.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="review-section" aria-labelledby="begin-title">
      <div class="review-section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="review-kicker">A place to begin</p>
          <h2 id="begin-title">Some questions refuse to stay in their assigned room.</h2>
        </div>
        <span class="review-status is-ember">building in public</span>
      </div>
      <div class="review-story-panel">
        <p>I have always been drawn to connections that did not seem to belong to the same category at first: computer science and psychology, research and making, code and music, structure and the sudden eureka of noticing that two ideas have been talking to each other all along. I could often see the route from one to another, and then lose weeks trying to find a form sturdy enough to carry all of it.</p>
        <p>That is the practical reason for Hearth &amp; Code. I want to do research carefully, with sources that can be checked and conclusions that can be challenged, and I also want the work to feel alive enough that the next surprising connection, rabbit hole, or small build can pull me back to the desk with genuine curiosity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="review-quote">
        <p>“I want the research to be serious enough to matter, and alive enough that I want to keep coming back to it.”</p>
        <small>{publicSite ? 'Shaped from Scott’s authoring material' : 'Local-review copy, shaped from Scott’s authoring material'}</small>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="review-section">
      <EcosystemProcessRail
        id="hearth-process"
        eyebrow="A public practice, made legible"
        title="Follow one question from first spark to a public record."
        description="This is not a workflow engine or a claim that every question follows a tidy sequence. It is a reader-controlled orientation to how public artifacts connect without exposing a private workspace."
        stages={ecosystemStages}
      />
    </div>

    <section class="review-section" aria-labelledby="rooms-title">
      <div class="review-section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="review-kicker">The rooms</p>
          <h2 id="rooms-title">One hearth, several rooms, and each room gets to do its own work.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="review-section-intro">I do not want a visitor to read the same explanation eight times. Each room opens one door, then points toward the next question if they want to keep going.</p>
      <div class="review-card-grid">
        {rooms.map((room) => (
          <article class="review-card">
            <p class="review-label">{room.label}</p>
            <h3>{room.title}</h3>
            <p>{room.copy}</p>
            {room.secondaryLink ? (
              <div class="review-card-links" aria-label="Field Journal reading views">
                <a href={room.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Article List ↗</a>
                <a href={room.secondaryLink.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{room.secondaryLink.label}</a>
              </div>
            ) : (
              <a href={room.href} target={room.external ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={room.external ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}>Enter this room{room.external ? ' ↗' : ' →'}</a>
            )}
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="review-section" aria-labelledby="invitation-title">
      <div class="review-split">
        <div>
          <p class="review-kicker">For the curious</p>
          <h3 id="invitation-title">If your questions have ever outgrown the room they were supposed to fit inside, you are welcome here.</h3>
          <p>The work is for people who have watched their own ideas get stranded between fascination and follow-through, for builders who want to inspect the source, and for anyone interested in what a serious, unromanticized collaboration between a person and an AI might make possible.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="review-kicker">Available now</p>
          <ul class="review-mini-list">
            <li><strong>Read the journal</strong>Follow the notes as the practice changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Inspect the source</strong>See the public repositories and small working proofs.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep a question</strong>Take the research question into your own work, with or without these tools.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</ReviewShell>
